many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism does stoicism  require repressing one's emotions this  is a great question  because it's so reflective of a major  misunderstanding a very widespread one  of what it is that stoic philosophy has  as its basis and what it's advocating  and what the goal or end point of  practicing stoicism is supposed to be  now if you're the sort of person who  wants a quick straightforward simple  answer the answer to this question is no  stoicism does not require repressing  one's emotions that's probably not that  satisfying of an answer because you  might say but I've but I heard that it  did or it seems from the things that  I've read that that's what one ought to  do so we want to look at what stoicism  actually tells us about the emotions  first though I think it's very useful to  make a distinction between lowercase s  stoicism which is a character trait or  disposition of toughness oftentimes  involving repressing emotions not  dealing with them not acknowledging them  that's not what we're talking about here  as uppercase s stoicism the philosophy  and set of practices that go all the way  back to ancient times let's start by  looking at what the Stoics have to say  about emotions in general and there's a  prevalent misunderstanding of them that  says that the Stoics thought all the  emotions were bad you can see people  repeating this in histories of  philosophy in interpretations of  stoicism and that's actually incorrect  if we look at what the Stoics did have  to say about emotions they thought that  many of our emotional responses are in  fact  bad they're negative they they don't  help us they actually are disturbances  of the soul or if you prefer a different  term the personality or the mind but the  Stoics also thought that there are some  emotional responses that are not just  neutral but are in fact good for us to  have they called these the new path AI  and those would be you know things like  rational amount of fear where they  called caution you LA baya rational  desire bool ASIS and they didn't talk  about about you know grief or pain in  that way but they did talk about a  rational type of pleasure sort of joy is  is the way that we could translate that  notice that I'm using the term rational  in all of those this doesn't mean that  we have to be you know totally in our  head and detached from our emotional  life what it means is that there are  certain limits or orientations or  guidelines given by our rational faculty  to how and why we're feeling the way  that we are so the Stoics think that we  should be joyful about some things we  should be cautious about some things we  should in fact feel confidence in some  cases we should in fact have desires now  that doesn't mean that that every single  joy or pleasure is automatically good  but they did distinguish that entire  range some of the others that they do  talk about are what we might call  attachment or familial affection Phil  Astoria Marcus will talk quite often  about about you know all sorts of other  emotions as soul Seneca many of them  will use the term love and in fact the  Stoics did not think that all love is is  necessarily pathological either so  there's a wide range of emotions that a  stoic wouldn't  press even if the Stoics were into  repressing emotions that leaves open of  course the question well what about  those bad emotions shouldn't we just  repress those and the Stoics would say  probably not because when you just  repress you push down an emotion you try  to you know submerge it below the level  of your consciousness or you resist it  and you you won't give in to it  you're not actually improving yourself  you are just taking a stand it could be  good if you're you know finding yourself  very angry to not say that thing that  you want to say but that's not  repressing the emotion that's just not  acting on that emotion to repress the  emotions means to tell yourself I'm not  going to feel this or I'm not feeling it  and you tell yourself that enough until  you actually believe that you're not  angry afraid you know sad whatever it  happens to be this is not a productive  way to approach things because if you do  that you're not changing anything about  the environment that is is you know  having you feel that way you're not  gaining any insight about why your  response to those triggers stressors  events is causing you to feel that  emotion so it's going to keep on  happening over and over and over again  and you're gonna find yourself having to  repress it even more and then it can  become a bad habit  that becomes quite difficult to break so  that's one big reason why it's not  helpful but it also tends to deform you  the idea behind stoic philosophy is  self-knowledge we want to understand  what's going on in our damaged screwed  up selves so that we can selectively  start to work on things and fix it if I  find that I'm really struggling with  anger it's not going to do me any good  to repress it I need to know why I'm  angry when I'm getting angry what it is  that I'm getting angry over I need to  look at my reactions and be honest with  myself perhaps even bring in a coach to  get somebody else to give me some  honesty only then can I begin to work  on those emotions so that I will feel  them less or less often or less  intensely or perhaps not even feel them  at all  that's what stoicism is suggesting in  terms of the disturbing emotions so it's  clear that we don't want to repress  emotions in stoicism where might you go  if you want to learn more about the  Stoics and emotion well there's  classical sources and I would actually  direct you to Cicero's Tusculum  disputations today I'd nasal air tease  lives of the philosophers book 7 chapter  1 about Zeno and the doctrines of the  Stoics and two arias did a mrs. epitome  of stoic ethics I would also read around  in Seneca in Epictetus and in marcus  aurelius and you're gonna find lots of  great discussions Seneca in fact has an  entire book on anger which could be very  helpful for you to read and there  happens to be one excellent contemporary  work by a scholar  on this very topic it's Margaret graver  and her stoicism and emotion it's a  somewhat difficult book it's written for  an academic audience but it's pretty  readable and she is dead on in her  analyses of the Stoics in their  discussions of emotion if you found this  answer helpful for you then please share  it with others who might benefit from it  this work is entirely supported by  crowdfunding on my patreon site so if  you find it valuable consider becoming a  supporter at patreon.com slash Sadler
 

